  "statement": "Current regulatory proposals are not safeguards against AI disruption, but rather performative 'safety washing' designed to insulate incumbent tech firms from accountability while codifying their market dominance. By framing governance as a technical problem requiring 'flexibility,' proponents create a regulatory environment that prioritizes the speed of deployment over the rights of the public. This approach echoes historical patterns of regulatory capture, where industry leaders use the shield of technical complexity to bypass democratic oversight and cement their monopolies. Legislators should view 'expert-led' governance as a signal of intent to exclude the very people who bear the costs of these systems.\n\nProposed guardrails often function as marketing-driven mechanisms that ignore the measurable, present-day harms of algorithmic deployment. We see this in the documented displacement of labor and the amplification of systemic bias in hiring, lending, and law enforcement. When policymakers allow corporations to self-certify their safety standards, they effectively trade public welfare for the promise of future innovation. Real accountability requires strict liability for harms and transparent, adversarial auditing, not the vague, flexible frameworks currently under discussion.\n\nPolicy must prioritize empirical, present-day harm over the speculative, utopian promises of AI developers. The assumption that government expertise is inherently neutral ignores the reality that technical literacy often serves the interests of those who own the infrastructure. If we assume that innovation is inherently synonymous with societal progress, we forfeit our right to regulate the machines that increasingly govern our social and economic lives. My position depends on the assumption that power, not technical capacity, is the primary driver of regulatory outcomes; if that were false and the state were truly capable of autonomous, neutral governance, I would shift toward advocating for more robust, state-run technical oversight rather than structural decentralization.",
