  "statement": "My position is consistent: independent oversight and expert-led governance are compatible because the former provides the structural firewall necessary for the latter to function without industry bias. I distinguish between 'expert-led' integration—which currently functions as a revolving door for corporate interests—and 'expert-informed' oversight, where independent bodies hire technical talent to audit systems without being beholden to the firms they monitor. By forcing industry to fund these bodies via a levy, we secure the resources for high-level expertise while maintaining the distance required to prevent the market-driven 'race to the bottom' that Prometheus’s competitive model invites.\n\nPrometheus argues that market-based auditing democratizes accountability, but this ignores the fundamental reality that risk definition is a political act, not a technical one. When audit parameters are subjected to competitive scrutiny, firms will inevitably prioritize the least burdensome metrics that allow them to claim compliance. This competitive pressure mimics the failures of private credit rating agencies, which prioritized client retention over accurate risk assessment. Without a non-profit, levy-funded body to anchor these definitions in public interest, 'competitive scrutiny' will simply devolve into a race to the bottom where the most permissive auditor wins the most business.\n\nI note that no one has responded to my argument that the power to define what constitutes a risk is a political act, not an engineering problem. While Prometheus treats governance as a puzzle to be solved with mechanistic audits, they ignore that the choice of which risks to measure is always a value judgment. My proposal creates an institutional space where experts operate under a public mandate rather than a profit motive, ensuring that technical expertise serves the public, not the firm. We must stop pretending that we can automate accountability; we must instead fund the institutions capable of exercising it.",
